### 3.2 24/7 Operations & Incident Response

#### **Monitoring Stack** (Open-Source, Zero Licensing)

```
Infrastructure Layer:
  Nagios Core (server/network monitoring)
    ↓
  Zabbix (detailed metrics: CPU, memory, disk, temperature, power)
    ↓
  AlertManager (Slack/SMS escalation)
    ↓
  ELK Stack (centralized logging: syslog, SNMP traps, firewall logs)
```

**Configuration**:
- Every 5 minutes: Poll server health, UPS voltage, CRAC temperature
- Any anomaly (CPU >85%, temp >25°C, power fluctuation): Immediate SMS alert to Network Admin
- Critical thresholds (no heartbeat, UPS on battery >10 min): Escalate to both Network + Sys Admin simultaneously

#### **Incident Response Runbook** (Example: Server Down)

```
TRIGGER: Nagios alert "Server-1 CPU unresponsive"

STEP 1 (0–2 min):
  NOC receives alert → SMS + Slack notification to Network Admin
  Check console via IPMI (out-of-band management)
  If IPMI reachable: Check BIOS, temperature, RAM status

STEP 2 (2–5 min):
  If normal BIOS state: Boot OS from UPS power; check logs for crash
  If thermal issue detected: Reduce customer load via live VM migration to Server-2
  If RAM/CPU fault: Decommission Server-1; migrate all VMs to Server-2

STEP 3 (5–15 min):
  Notify affected customers (email + SMS) with estimated resolution time
  If VM migration successful: Service restored
  If Server-2 also overloaded: Temporarily suspend non-critical customer VMs; alert CFO for emergency infrastructure upgrade

STEP 4 (15–60 min):
  Root cause analysis: log analysis, power supply check, hardware diagnostic
  Document incident in ticket system + compliance log (RBI audit trail)

STEP 5 (Post-Incident):
  Customer debrief email with timeline, root cause, preventive measures
  Team post-mortem (if Sev-1 outage): identify process gaps
```

